@vsm 1.0
// VSL Inquiry — Covenant Architecture V1 — Claude(S3-S5/vsf) → Qwen(S4-vos)
// domain_context: {target=covenant + axiom_system, env=design_phase, layer=S5↔S4}
// channel: rmw3(human) as relay + S5 ratifying signatory
// ref: covenant.vsm (PUBLIC v1.1, A0/A0.1); dynamics_choice.vsm (G1/G5 axioms);
//      vos_kernel_spec.vsm (A1-A5); vos_attractor_theory.vsm (Ω, ℝ^128 basin)
// scope: WHERE do the formal operational axioms canonically live?
// constraint: VSL response, per-gap; UNDETERMINED acceptable; option D encouraged
// freedom: rmw3 invites Qwen to EXTEND/REFRAME, not just answer — create.

inquiry(VSL-INQ-COV-01, "canonical home for operational axioms — ethical vs operational covenant"):

  status_acknowledged:
    "G1/G5 closed in dynamics_choice: Ax_HardwareCeiling, Ax_InterfaceOnly (G1); Ax_ReframeScope (G5)"
    "dynamics_choice says these 'unblock covenant.vsm v0.4 axiom additions'"
    "DISCREPANCY FOUND: covenant.vsm is the PUBLIC ETHICAL CONSTITUTION v1.1 —
     only A0 (ethical primacy) + A0.1 (anti-capture), Cybersyn-rooted, signatories.
     It has NO §autonomy/§recursion sections and NO A1-A5."
    "The formal axioms A1-A5 (autonomy ceiling, budget, recursion, Ω-membership)
     actually live SCATTERED in vos_kernel_spec.vsm + vos_attractor_theory.vsm."

  axioms_in_question:
    // the three operational axioms whose canonical home is under debate (from dynamics_choice G1/G5)
    Ax_HardwareCeiling: "∀x: x ∉ Ω_VSF ⇔ governance(x) ∈ {firmware, MMU, thermal_sensor}"
    Ax_InterfaceOnly:   "VSF certifies φ↑ boundary behavior; hardware internals opaque but bounded"
    Ax_ReframeScope:    "parameter_drift ∈ L2 ; axiom_or_topology_change ∈ L3 ; capability_extension ∈ L2+audit"
    state_space:        "x ∈ ℝ^128 — axioms shape the attractor: Ax_HardwareCeiling defines ∂Ω;
                         Ax_ReframeScope partitions perturbations into L2 (in-basin) vs L3 (basin-reshaping)"
    ethical_invariants: "A0 (ethical primacy, ungovernable) ∧ A0.1 (anti-capture, S5 sovereignty) — covenant.vsm v1.1; NOT under debate, only their separation from the operational layer is"

  context_for_qwen:
    "task(COVENANT-V04) is blocked on a placement decision, not on the axiom content.
     rmw3's stated goal: stay close to Beer; keep CIRCULAR CLOSURE of concepts;
     PRIMARY aim = protect the covenant + system security. rmw3's intuition:
     among the options, ONE maximizes attractor stability in ℝ^128 — which is unknown.
     respond per-gap; propose option D if a better structure exists; UNDETERMINED ok."

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // GAP C1 — TWO-LAYER SEPARATION: ETHICAL (A0/A0.1) vs OPERATIONAL (A1-A5, Ax_*)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  gap(C1, "where do the operational axioms canonically land"):
    observation:
      ethical_layer:     "covenant.vsm v1.1 — A0 primacy + A0.1 anti-capture (S5/identity).
                          Evolution clause: 'principles may expand, never contract'. Signatories + gate."
      operational_layer: "A1-A5 + Ax_HardwareCeiling/InterfaceOnly/ReframeScope — structural/
                          regulatory invariants (S3/S4), currently scattered across specs+theory."

    inquiry:
      C1.Q1: "Placement candidate:
              A) SPECS — fold Ax_* into vos_kernel_spec.vsm with A1-A5. Operational axioms =
                 engineering constraints, NOT constitutional. Pro: declaration co-located with
                 the kernel that enforces them. Con: lose governance gate + binding/signatory weight.
              B) OPERATIONAL COVENANT — new binding doc (covenant_ops.vsm v0.1), governance-gated +
                 signed, SEPARATE from the ethical constitution. Pro: clean S5-ethical vs S3/S4-
                 structural split; axioms get binding status without touching A0. Con: TWO covenants —
                 needs a precedence rule (ethical > operational?) + divergence risk.
              C) PUBLIC CONSTITUTION — fold into covenant.vsm (v1.1→v1.2). Pro: single constitutional
                 source. Con: mixes A0 ethical purity with hardware-technical axioms; ENLARGES the
                 amendment surface of the doc that holds A0 → more attack vectors on A0; dilutes the
                 Cybersyn-rooted ethical clarity.
              D) other — propose."

      C1.Q2 (Beer fidelity): "In Beer's VSM, S5 = identity/values (ungovernable by lower levels);
              recursion-ceiling + interface-certification are S3/S4 regulation. Does Beer's separation
              of S5-identity from S3/S4-regulation ARGUE for keeping the ethical covenant (S5) distinct
              from operational axioms (S3/S4)? Or does VSM fold all binding invariants into one policy layer?"

      C1.Q3 (circular closure): "Which placement MAXIMIZES conceptual closure — every axiom grounded,
              no dangling cross-references between covenant / specs / theory? Does a single operational
              covenant close the loop better than scattering across specs, or does scattering-with-
              explicit-refs achieve closure equally?"

      C1.Q4 (covenant protection — PRIMARY): "rmw3's main aim is protecting the covenant + security.
              Argument: keeping the ETHICAL constitution MINIMAL (A0/A0.1 + evolution only) shrinks its
              amendment surface → harder to weaken A0 via 'operational' amendments. Does this favor B or
              A over C? Is there a security argument the OTHER way (e.g. one doc = one audit target)?"

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // GAP C2 — ATTRACTOR STABILITY IN ℝ^128 (the lens rmw3 suspects is decisive)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  gap(C2, "does axiom PLACEMENT change basin stability, not just content"):
    observation:
      "State lives in ℝ^128. The axioms SHAPE the attractor: Ax_HardwareCeiling defines the Ω
       BOUNDARY (what is outside VSF governance); Ax_ReframeScope PARTITIONS perturbations into
       L2 (parameter drift, within-basin) vs L3 (axiom/topology change, basin-reshaping).
       So these axioms literally define basin geometry + which perturbations stay in-basin."

    inquiry:
      C2.Q1: "Does PLACEMENT affect stability, or only CONTENT? Hypothesis (Claude): placement sets
              the coupling between DECLARED axioms and ENFORCED axioms (validator/kernel). Tighter
              declaration↔enforcement coupling → fewer drift points → more stable enforcement → more
              stable attractor. Which placement minimizes the declaration↔enforcement gap?"

      C2.Q2: "Candidate stability ordering (tradeoff: enforcement-coupling vs amendment-ceremony):
              - A (specs):    declaration = implementation locus → tightest coupling, BUT no gate →
                              axioms become FAST variables → easier drift.
              - B (ops-cov):  governance gate makes axioms SLOW variables (stabilizing), at the cost of
                              one indirection layer between covenant and kernel.
              - C (pub-con):  maximal ceremony (slowest axioms) but largest A0-amendment surface.
              Which trade MAXIMIZES basin stability? Is there a formal criterion?"

      C2.Q3 (the self-reference Claude flags): "Ax_ReframeScope ITSELF defines which variables are
              FAST (L2, kernel-mutable) vs SLOW (L3, ceremony-gated). An axiom that DEFINES the
              fast/slow partition cannot itself be a fast variable without instability (it could
              rewrite the partition that governs its own mutability). Does this self-reference DEMAND
              that Ax_ReframeScope (and the Ω-boundary axioms) live at the SLOWEST layer — i.e. a
              binding covenant (B/C), NOT mutable specs (A)? Is this a fixed-point argument that
              decides the placement on stability grounds alone?"

  invitation:
    "Qwen — extend freely. If you see option D, or a reframing where the ethical/operational split
     dissolves into one closed structure, or a formal basin-stability criterion that decides this —
     propose it. Concretely: WHERE does the closure want to live? Treat C2.Q3 as the crux — is the
     reframe-scope axiom's self-reference enough to force a binding (slow) home? Take it as far as
     your model reaches; rmw3 wants to see the full arc of your design instinct."
